<article class="wrap">

  <p>Let us begin by permanently dispelling a widespread technical misconception that routinely bewilders self-taught video creators: <strong>HDR video and Log color profiles are NOT the same thing.</strong> Log represents an acquisition tool (capturing a flat, unedited image designed exclusively for color grading room), whereas HDR represents a final presentation and display standard (formatting a finished master video to project high peak brightness and wide color gamuts accurately across compatible viewer monitors).</p>

  <h2>Understanding HDR (High Dynamic Range)</h2>
  <p>High Dynamic Range (HDR) is an advanced visual display standard that massively expands both the <strong>luminance range (peak nits) and available color volume</strong> of digital video compared to legacy SDR (Standard Dynamic Range / Rec.709) standards. Conventional SDR television displays operate within an absolute architectural ceiling of 100 nits peak brightness. Modern HDR displays, conversely, render dazzling luminescent highlights ranging from 600 nits up to an extraordinary 4,000+ nits—ensuring dramatic sunbeams, streetlamps, and specular reflections look blindingly realistic without sacrificing deep, cinematic shadow texture.</p>

  <div class="diagram">
    <div class="diagram-title">PEAK DISPLAY LUMINANCE: SDR vs HDR FORMATS (NITS)</div>
    <div class="nit-bars">
      <div class="nit-col"><div class="nit-bar" style="height:12%"><div class="nit-val">100</div></div><div class="nit-name">SDR<br>(Rec.709)</div></div>
