  <h2>The 4 Dominant HDR Formats</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Format Type</th><th>Metadata Encoding</th><th>Bit Depth Standard</th><th>Primary Industry Deployment</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>HDR10</b></td><td>Static (One universal maximum exposure setting per video file)</td><td>10-bit</td><td>Open universal standard—supported across almost all Smart TVs, YouTube, and Blu-ray media</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>HDR10+</b></td><td>Dynamic (Adjusts brightness scaling scene-by-scene)</td><td>10-bit</td><td>Royalty-free advanced standard developed by Samsung and Amazon Prime Video</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Dolby Vision</b></td><td>Dynamic (Precise frame-by-frame computational brightness scaling)</td><td>10-bit to 12-bit</td><td>Premium licensed cinema standard utilized by Apple, Netflix, and Disney+</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>HLG</b> (Hybrid Log-Gamma)</td><td>Zero external metadata required</td><td>10-bit</td><td>Joint broadcast standard (BBC/NHK) engineered for seamless SDR backward-compatibility</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><strong>HLG stands as the most practical format for fast television and internet broadcasting</strong> because it operates without requiring external dynamic metadata files. An HLG signal can be directly broadcast to both classic legacy SDR televisions (where it renders as normal footage) and modern HDR OLED monitors (where it expands to dazzling luminance) from a single unified transmission file.</p>

  <h2>How HDR Video Operates Inside iPhones</h2>
  <div class="brand-card">
    <h4>Dolby Vision Profile 8.4</h4>
    <p>Starting from the iPhone 12 series onward, whenever a user activates "HDR Video" inside their iOS settings menu, the device begins capturing immersive <strong>Dolby Vision Profile 8.4</strong> recordings. This specialized Apple profile leverages a 10-bit HEVC (H.265) compression architecture wrapped atop an underlying HLG color matrix. Using its powerful Bionic neural engines, the iPhone analyzes scene contrast in real-time, injecting frame-by-frame dynamic metadata directly into the recorded video container for automatic high-impact display playback.</p>
    <span class="spec-chip">10-Bit HEVC (H.265)</span><span class="spec-chip">Frame-by-Frame Metadata</span><span class="spec-chip">iPhone 12 through 16 Series</span>
  </div>
  <div class="callout"><b>Critical Workflow Distinction:</b> Never confuse standard iPhone Dolby Vision HDR recording with professional <em>Apple Log</em> video acquisition. Dolby Vision creates a finished, high-contrast, baked-in HDR visual presentation ready for immediate social media uploading. Apple Log (restricted to Pro models using compatible camera applications) generates an uncompressed, flat, desaturated capture profile intended exclusively for manual desktop editing and color styling.</div>

  <h2>How Dedicated Cinema and Mirrorless Cameras Handle HDR</h2>
  <p>Professional camera architectures typically offer two primary workflows for generating HDR material:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>HLG Picture Profiles:</strong> Sony, Panasonic, and Fujifilm cameras allow operators to select HLG gamma profiles natively in-camera. This produces a resilient 10-bit signal that requires minimal grading time while preserving backward compatibility with legacy television panels.</li>
    <li><strong>Canon HDR-PQ Mode:</strong> Advanced Canon bodies feature dedicated HDR PQ (Perceptual Quantizer / Rec.2100-PQ) recording modes designed to capture immediate high-luminance video files ready for playback on calibrated reference HDR grading broadcast monitors.</li>
  </ul>

